# Rating component pattern

React Spectrum has no `Rating` component. When building one, follow this **team
decision** about its accessibility model — it is not obvious and the AI should not
guess.

## Accessibility model: radiogroup, not slider
- Render the rating as a **`radiogroup`** (`role="radiogroup"` with an accessible
  name), where **each star is one radio option** with its own label, e.g.
  "1 Star", "2 Stars". Exactly one option is checked.
- This wins over a `slider` because each value gets a discrete, individually labelled,
  keyboard-focusable target and announces cleanly in screen readers.

## Required behavior
- **Keyboard:** Left/Right (and Up/Down) arrows move between stars; Home/End jump to
  first/last; the selected star is in the tab order (roving tabindex via the radio
  group primitive).
- **Hover preview:** hovering a star previews that value without committing until
  press; clearing hover restores the committed value.
- **Half stars:** support a `0.5` step via an `allowHalf` prop when requested.
- **Controlled & uncontrolled:** support `value` / `defaultValue` / `onChange`.

## Read-only display mode
- When `isReadOnly` is set, do **not** render interactive radios. Render a static
  presentation with `role="img"` and an `aria-label` summarizing the value, e.g.
  `aria-label="Rated 3 out of 5 stars"`.

## Styling & structure
- Follow `s2-component-authoring`: build on React Aria primitives, `forwardRef` +
  named export, style via the `style` macro with tokens (no raw colors), and co-locate
  `stories/Rating.stories.tsx` + `test/Rating.test.tsx`.
